<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Picts</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Picts were the people of northern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in late antiquity and the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, first named by a Roman writer in 297. Descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> Caledonians and other tribes, they spoke a Brittonic language and left carved symbol stones. Their kingdoms, chiefly Fortriu, merged with the Gaelic kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> in the 9th century to form Alba, the later Kingdom of Scotland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/picts.jpg" alt="Picts" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: D Lloyd · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Serpent_stone.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The community of Iona, founded under the kingdom’s protection, carried Christianity to the <b>Picts</b> and to the English: in 635 the Iona monk Aidan founded Lindisfarne at the invitation of King Oswald of Northumbria, who had spent his exile among the Gaels, and bishops trained in the Iona…</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Ecgfrith extended rule over the <b>Picts</b> and established a bishopric at Abercorn on the Forth, but lost Lindsey after defeat by Æthelred of Mercia at the Battle of the Trent in 679.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Scotland grew out of the union of the <b>Picts</b>, who held most of the land north of the Forth, and the Gaelic kingdom of Dál Riata in Argyll.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-scotland">Kingdom of Scotland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Picts</b> were the peoples of Scotland north of the Forth and Clyde in late Roman and early medieval times.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>From the late 3rd century Britain faced growing raids by Saxons and Franks from across the North Sea, <b>Picts</b> from the north and Scotti from Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman defences against them included the forts of the Saxon Shore in Britain and on the Gallic coast, named in the Notitia Dignitatum, and in 367 Saxons joined <b>Picts</b>, Scotti and Attacotti in the attack on Britain that Ammianus Marcellinus called a barbarian conspiracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Military</a></li></ul></section>
